      "worked_solution": "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,3).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(3) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(3)=2.$",
